            <ArticleTitle>Crosstalk between Diverse Synthetic Protein Degradation Tags in Escherichia coli.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1021/acssynbio.7b00122</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently, a synthetic circuit in E. coli demonstrated that two proteins engineered with LAA tags targeted to the native protease ClpXP are susceptible to crosstalk due to competition for degradation between proteins. To understand proteolytic crosstalk beyond the single protease regime, we investigated in E. coli a set of synthetic circuits designed to probe the dynamics of existing and novel degradation tags fused to fluorescent proteins. These circuits were tested using both microplate reader and single-cell assays. We first quantified the degradation rates of each tag in isolation. We then tested if there was crosstalk between two distinguishable fluorescent proteins engineered with identical or different degradation tags. We demonstrated that proteolytic crosstalk was indeed not limited to the LAA degradation tag, but was also apparent between other diverse tags, supporting the complexity of the E. coli protein degradation system.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
